CORE
JmlMapLibrary
--function "JmlMapLibrary.useIt:()I" --jml-source src/main/java/JmlMapLibrary.java --axiomatic-collections --unwind 5 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for JML library-call recognition: `m.size()`
and `m.isEmpty()` for HashMap / Map / Set receivers
inside JML clauses.

Pipeline
--------
  1. Source extractor parses the parameter list with proper
     `<>` nesting (so HashMap<Integer, Integer> m extracts
     a single param named `m`, not two malformed pieces).

  2. resolve_jml_expr's library-call branch (added on this
     feature branch) sees `jml_method_call(size, [m])` or
     `jml_method_call(isEmpty, [m])` with a Map-shaped
     receiver, looks up the global `_sz` symbol installed
     by the --axiomatic-collections lowering pass, and
     emits the equivalent inline expression
       index_exprt(_sz, pack_receiver(m))
     for `size()`, or `index == 0` for `isEmpty()`.

  3. Library-call recognition runs BEFORE trivial-getter
     rewrite so collection method calls don't fall through
     to the "non-trivial method call" warning.

The test exercises the call-graph case: `useIt()` allocates
a fresh HashMap (which the axiomatic <init> sets
`_sz[handle] := 0`), then calls `sizeOfEmpty` whose
precondition `m.isEmpty()` resolves to `_sz[handle] == 0`,
which holds. Verification SUCCESSFUL.

What this does NOT yet cover (deferred to follow-up):
  * Map.containsKey, Map.get — need per-receiver/per-key
    indexing; designed but not implemented.
  * Set.contains — same dependency as containsKey.
  * Map<K, V>.size on the abstract Map interface (works for
    HashMap concrete; abstract is a follow-up if needed).
  * List.size, List.isEmpty — JBMC does not yet axiomatically
    model ArrayList/LinkedList. Would need parallel work.
